---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Setup guide">
  <article class="guide wrap">
    <div class="guide-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE / GETTING STARTED</p><h1>A home for the Athan.</h1><p class="lede">
        What you need, which cable goes where, and how to finish setting up your OpenAthan.
      </p>
    </div><div class="guide-body">
      <aside class="notice">
        <strong>First-time installation is not available yet.</strong><p>
          OpenAthan is in early development. The installer will open when tested firmware and
          approved recordings are published. The current preview does not establish release
          readiness.
        </p>
      </aside><h2>Start with the reference hardware</h2><p>
        This preview supports the <strong>M5Stack AtomS3R C126</strong> and <strong
          >Pyramid A167</strong
        > speaker base. M5Stack calls the A167 “Echo Pyramid”; OpenAthan also refers to it as Voice Pyramid.
        Other ESP32 boards are not supported by this installer.
      </p><p>
        You will need a computer with desktop Chrome or Edge, a USB-C <strong>data</strong> cable, and
        a 2.4 GHz WPA2/WPA3 personal Wi-Fi network. A charging-only cable will not work for USB setup.
        Enterprise networks and networks with a sign-in page are not supported.
      </p><h2>1. Connect through the Atom</h2><p>
        Attach the modules using their intended connector. Disconnect bottom power, then connect the
        computer to the <strong>Atom’s USB-C port</strong>. Use only this one cable during USB
        setup.
      </p><p>
        Close other serial tools and browser tabs using the device. On <a href="/install/"
          >the installer page</a
        >, choose your path and select the USB JTAG/serial device when prompted.
      </p><h2>2. Install, or recover an existing device</h2><p>
        A new installation replaces the device’s existing firmware and data. It is not a
        settings-preserving update. If OpenAthan is already installed, choose <strong
          >Fix Wi-Fi or password</strong
        >. Recovery preserves your prayer settings and playback history.
      </p><h2>3. Save Wi-Fi and a device password</h2><p>
        Select your home network or enter its name for a hidden network, then enter its Wi-Fi
        password. Next, create a separate, long device password. Your browser sends these
        credentials directly over USB; the website does not store them.
      </p><p>
        Remember the device password. You will use the username <strong>admin</strong> to sign into the
        device’s settings.
      </p><h2>4. Move the power cable</h2><p>
        When USB setup finishes, unplug the Atom’s USB cable. Connect <strong
          >only the Pyramid’s bottom USB-C power</strong
        > for normal speaker operation. Wait for Wi-Fi to reconnect.
      </p><h2>5. Finish on the device</h2><p>
        Keep your computer or phone on the same home network and open the device address shown by
        the installer, such as <code>http://openathan-a1b2c3.local/</code>. Each device has its own
        address. If the hostname does not work, try its displayed IP address.
      </p><p>
        Sign in, enter your location and timezone, choose a calculation method, and review the
        prayer times. Select <strong>Finish setup</strong> to activate announcements. If the clock has
        not synchronized yet, announcements will wait.
      </p><h2>Everyday use</h2><p>
        Your device calculates prayer times and plays its stored recordings without this website or
        Home Assistant. Wi-Fi is used to synchronize its clock and open its settings page. After a
        cold boot it needs a usable time source before it can announce prayers. Keep the local
        settings page on your trusted home network; do not expose it to the Internet.
      </p><div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/install/">Open device setup <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        ><a href="/guides/recovery/">Troubleshooting & recovery</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
